      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The effect of acetylsalicylic acid (ASA) on capsaicin-evoked activation of cutaneous nociceptors was tested in a double blind study in 10 volunteers. Capsaicin (2% in ethanol) was applied topically for 30 min. Topical ASA (0.25 g/ml) reduced pain intensity and axon reflex flare size. Also, areas of secondary hyperalgesia to light touch and pin-prick were diminished. In contrast, capsaicin-induced heat hyperalgesia was unaffected by ASA. It is concluded that ASA counteracts the excitatory effects of capsaicin on nociceptors and mechanical hyperalgesia but not its sensitizing action to heat."},
